+<!-- data_types.data_declarations.lazy_data -->
+#### 10.1.1B Lazy data declarations and enterable head semantics
+
+A `lazy data` declaration opts one algebraic data family into enterable-head semantics.
+
+Example:
+
+```kappa
+lazy data List (a : Type) : Type =
+    Nil
+    Cons a (List a)
+
+lazy data StrictHeadList (a : Type) : Type =
+    Nil
+    Cons (!head : a) (tail : StrictHeadList a)
+```
+
+The modifier is local to the declared data family. It does not make every type in the language lazy, does not create
+lazy function arrows, and does not rewrite constructor fields to `Thunk` or `Need`.
+
+Conceptual hidden representation:
+
+For a declaration:
+
+```kappa
+lazy data T Δ : Type =
+    C1 fields1...
+    C2 fields2...
+```
+
+elaboration behaves as if the implementation creates a hidden head representation:
+
+```text
+T# Δ =
+    C1# fields1...
+    C2# fields2...
+```
+
+and treats source values of type `T Δ` as enterable cells producing exactly one `T# Δ` head.
+
+This notation is explanatory only:
+
+* `T#` is not a source-visible type constructor;
+* `C1#`, `C2#`, ... are not source-visible constructors;
+* source pattern matching sees only the declared source constructors `C1`, `C2`, ...;
+* the hidden delayed state is not a constructor and never participates in exhaustiveness checking.
+
+A runtime value of lazy data type `T Δ` is in exactly one of the following observable implementation states:
+
+```text
+evaluated head : T# Δ
+delayed head   : memoized computation producing T# Δ
+```
+
+The delayed state stores a computation that produces one constructor head, not a value of type `T Δ`. Thus a lazy data
+cell is not a recursive chain of hidden `Delay (T Δ)` wrappers. Operationally, entering a lazy data cell performs one
+outer representation check; if the cell is delayed, the implementation evaluates the stored head computation, updates
+the cell with the resulting head when sharing is used, and returns that head.
+
+The delayed computation may itself perform arbitrary total computation before producing the head. That internal
+computation may enter other lazy values, call functions, allocate, or pattern match. The invariant is only that the cell
+being entered is updated with one `T# Δ` head, not with another hidden delayed `T Δ` value.
+
+Hidden operation:
+
+```text
+enter_T : T Δ -> T# Δ
+```
+
+Pattern matching, constructor-tag tests, and constructor-field projection on a lazy data family implicitly enter the
+scrutinee enough to reveal one hidden head. For example:
+
+```kappa
+match xs
+case Nil -> e_nil
+case Cons h t -> e_cons
+```
+
+behaves as if it first performed `enter_List xs` and then matched the resulting `List#` head. No source branch for the
+hidden delayed state is permitted or required.
+
+Source constructor types:
+
+Source constructors keep their ordinary source types. For the list example:
+
+```kappa
+Nil  : List a
+Cons : a -> List a -> List a
+```
+
+The field types are exactly the declared types. In particular, `Cons` does not have source type:
+
+```kappa
+Need a -> Need (List a) -> List a
+```
+
+unless the declaration explicitly writes such field types with ordinary `lazy` suspension-binder sugar.
+
+Constructor application and field demand:
+
+* Creating or entering a lazy data constructor head does not WHNF-normalize unmarked fields.
+* A field marked `!` is WHNF-entered before it is stored in the hidden constructor head.
+* The `!` marker does not force through `Thunk A` or `Need A`; it normalizes the field value itself to WHNF at its field
+  type.
+* Constructor defaults follow the same rule: an omitted default for a `!` field is WHNF-entered before storage, while an
+  omitted default for an unmarked field is not.
+* Putting an already constructed value into a field does not create a new suspension merely because the field is
+  unmarked. Capture happens when a delayed constructor-head computation is formed, not when a field slot receives an
+  already existing value.
+
+Lazy-data delay formation:
+
+The surface expression:
+
+```kappa
+lazy expr
+```
+
+keeps the suspension meaning of §16.2.2 when it synthesizes a type, or when it is checked against an expected type
+`Need A`.
+
+Additionally, when `lazy expr` is checked against an expected type whose head is known to be a `lazy data` family
+application `T Δ`, it may elaborate to a hidden delayed `T Δ` cell. The delayed computation is checked in a
+head-demanding context for `T# Δ`: running it evaluates `expr` far enough to produce one hidden constructor head for
+`T Δ`.
+
+This rule is expected-type-directed. If the expected type is absent or is not known to be a lazy data family, `lazy expr`
+does not silently produce a value of arbitrary type `A`; it uses the ordinary `Need A` rule of §16.2.2 or is rejected.
+
+Abstract and polymorphic lazy data:
+
+The compiler may form a lazy-data delay only when the expected type is known to carry lazy-data metadata. This knowledge
+may come from:
+
+* the concrete data-family head in the current module;
+* an imported module interface that exports the lazy-data capability for an opaque or abstract data family;
+* an `expect lazy data` declaration satisfied by an artifact that records compatible lazy-data metadata; or
+* explicit compiler-recognized evidence required by a future extension.
+
+Kappa does not infer this capability from erased type arguments at runtime. If source code is polymorphic over
+`a : Type` and attempts to form `lazy expr : a`, the program is rejected unless the type carries explicit lazy-data
+capability evidence in the source interface. The compiler is not permitted to perform runtime representation guessing
+based only on an erased type parameter.
+
+Quantities, captures, and positive lower bounds:
+
+A delayed lazy-data head is a closure boundary for quantity and capture checking.
+
+* Constructing a delayed head does not demand the variables mentioned by its body.
+* Uses inside the delayed head do not discharge positive lower-bound obligations unless the lazy data cell is entered
+  sufficiently before those obligations leave scope.
+* A delayed head may not allow a borrowed view or anonymous region to escape the region that validates it.
+* A delayed head that is memoized must not duplicate or retain linear or borrowed state except as allowed by the ordinary
+  quantity, borrow, capture, and memoization rules.
+
+These obligations attach to the delayed constructor-head computation. They do not attach to constructor fields merely
+because those fields are unmarked. Once a head is in WHNF, its fields are ordinary stored values of their declared
+source types.
+
+Totality and productivity:
+
+`lazy data` does not license arbitrary recursive definitions.
+
+A recursive delayed lazy-data definition is accepted only when the ordinary termination/productivity checker accepts the
+recursive SCC. Unguarded lazy recursion such as:
+
+```kappa
+bad : List Int
+let bad = lazy bad
+```
+
+is rejected unless a future extension explicitly supplies a productive interpretation. Guarded productive definitions
+such as cyclic or infinite lists must pass the same guarded-corecursion or productivity discipline used by the rest of
+Kappa.
+
+Constructor metadata preservation:
+
+Lazy-data constructor behavior is constructor metadata. It is preserved by the same metadata-preserving paths as named
+constructor application metadata:
+
+* direct constructor references;
+* transparent local bindings whose right-hand side is a single metadata-preserving constructor value;
+* record or package fields whose stored value records the metadata;
+* projection from such fields; and
+* import/export through module interfaces that record the metadata.
+
+Eta-expansion, ordinary lambda wrapping, sealing behind a signature that omits constructor metadata, conversion to an
+ordinary function type that does not record metadata, or storage through a field whose interface omits metadata destroys
+lazy-data constructor metadata. Positional application of the resulting ordinary function remains governed by ordinary
+function application.
+
+Definitional equality and normalization:
+
+The hidden delayed state of a lazy data value is not a source constructor and is not part of source-level pattern
+coverage. Definitional equality may enter a lazy data value only when the ordinary normalization rules require a
+constructor head and the delayed head computation is accepted as total and reducible in that normalization context.
+Entering a lazy data value reveals at most one hidden head for the current scrutinee.
+
+Implementation latitude:
+
+An implementation may choose whether a direct lazy-data constructor application allocates an already evaluated head cell
+or a delayed head cell, provided it preserves the source-visible demand behavior above. It may use update-in-progress
+markers, indirections, pointer tags, or other runtime machinery internally. Such machinery is not part of the source
+semantics and does not add constructors to the source data family.

