+<!-- compiler.kcore.lazy_data_head_cells -->
+#### 30.2.2A KCore lazy-data head cells
+
+A conforming implementation that accepts `lazy data` MUST behave as if each lazy data family has a hidden head
+representation and an enter operation.
+
+For a source declaration:
+
+```kappa
+lazy data T Δ : Type =
+    C1 fields1...
+    C2 fields2...
+```
+
+KCore behaves as if it contains a compiler-private head representation:
+
+```text
+T# Δ = C1# fields1... | C2# fields2...
+```
+
+and compiler-private operations observationally equivalent to:
+
+```text
+DelayHead_T : Need (T# Δ) -> T Δ
+Enter_T     : T Δ -> T# Δ
+```
+
+`T#`, `C#`, `DelayHead_T`, and `Enter_T` are explanatory names for required behavior. They are not source names, are not
+ordinary importable declarations, and need not exist with these spellings in an implementation.
+
+Rules:
+
+* `DelayHead_T` stores a memoized computation producing one hidden head `T# Δ`, not a computation producing `T Δ`.
+* `Enter_T` performs one outer enter operation on the supplied `T Δ` cell. If the cell already stores a head, that head
+  is returned. If the cell stores a delayed head computation, the computation is evaluated, the cell is updated with the
+  produced head when sharing is used, and that head is returned.
+* Pattern matching, constructor-tag tests, and constructor-field projections on `T Δ` elaborate through `Enter_T` before
+  selecting among the hidden heads.
+* `Enter_T` is not `Force` on `Need (T Δ)`. Lazy data does not add a source-visible suspension wrapper to the data type.
+* Ordinary `Thunk A` / `Need A` values remain governed by §30.2.2.
+
+
